{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfBV6aJHZYTcjcyPDdhs3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted index 21 (La₀.₄₉Sb₀.₅₁, near the composition LaSbO₄) as the best composition. The catch is that it barely responds to light: its EQE was about 0.0007 %, which is within noise of zero.\n\n**Merit measurements (3 of 3 used):**\n\n| Index | Sb fraction | EQE (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 21 | 0.51 | +0.0007 |\n| 12 | 0.26 | −0.0001 |\n| 27 | 0.66 | −0.0008 |\n\n**XRD (4 of 5 used):** I scanned indices 4, 12, 21 and 27, at Sb fractions of 0.12 to 0.66. The film peaks got stronger with more Sb. The two strong peaks that appear in every scan, at Q = 4.55 and 5.33 Å⁻¹, are probably from the substrate. This told me how the film changes along the line, but not where the photoactive composition would be. So I spread the three merit measurements across the line instead of concentrating them in one region.\n\nAll three results are essentially zero, so this La–Sb oxide line looks inactive at 3.2 eV illumination. Picking index 21 over the other two means little when all three are this close to zero. I didn't measure merit on the La-rich end (Sb below 0.2). I expect it to be inactive too, because La₂O₃-like phases have band gaps too wide to absorb 3.2 eV light, but that isn't tested. The submission was accepted."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":574,"cache_read_input_tokens":9462,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":574},"output_tokens":498,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d6f4c2b0-d56a-4069-ac1e-9822a43230af","uuid":"4a2b1ecb-7bbc-4db5-b183-c71e7bf5d698","timestamp":"2026-09-18T19:49:54.061Z","request_id":"req_011CfBV6ZvDgNyFtmBHwcpJf"}
